  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Fatehlal vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:13596) on 20 March, 2024",
    "facts": "As per the prosecution, on 15.03.1996, certain milk samples were taken from two drums in possession of the petitioner who is a milk vendor. The report of the milk samples prepared by the public analyst, Jaipur, in conformity with the procedure provided under Prevention of Food Adulteration Act revealed that the milk [2024:RJ-JD:13596] (2 of 4) [CRLR-981/2002] found in the possession of the petitioner was 'Adulterated'. The petitioner was thereafter prosecuted for the offence under Section 7/16 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 and sentenced as above.",
    "arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner has been falsely implicated in the present case. There is no positive evidence available on record indicating that the petitioner is guilty Fatehlal vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:13596) on 20 March, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/26815335/ 1 of committing the alleged crime. Learned counsel vehemently submitted that as a matter of fact, the milk was not for general sale but the same was being taken to the factory where the petitioner was a supervisor, for a tea party. Learned counsel further submitted that merely on the basis of the report of public analyst, it cannot be said that the petitioner had committed the alleged crime or that the milk was 'adulterated'. Lastly, on the strength of the aforesaid arguments and the facts and circumstances of the above case, learned counsel submitted that since the incident in the present case relates to the year 1996 and since the petitioner does not have any criminal antecedents, therefore, the present revision petition deserves acceptance. Learned counsel for the petitioner in the alternative submitted that the occurrence relates to the year 1996 and the petitioner has already served some part of the sentence awarded to him, therefore, the substantive sentence awarded to the petitioner may be reduced to the period already undergone by him. [2024:RJ-JD:13596] (3 of 4) [CRLR-981/2002] Reliance was placed on the judgment of the Hon'ble Supreme Court of India in the case of Puttaswamy v State of Karnataka: 2009 (1) WLC (SC) (Cri.) 623 and a judgment of the Coordinate Bench of this Court in the case of Kamla Prasad v. State of Rajasthan: 2014 CrilLJ 2582. Per contra, learned Public Prosecutor submitted that there is no illegality or infirmity in the impugned judgments whatsoever and therefore, the same do not call for any interference in exercise of revisional jurisdiction by this Court.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Learned counsel for the petitioner submitted that the petitioner has been falsely implicated in the present case. There is no positive evidence available on record indicating that the petitioner is guilty Fatehlal vs State (2024:Rj-Jd:13596) on 20 March, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/26815335/ 1 of committing the alleged crime. Learned counsel vehemently submitted that as a matter of fact, the milk was not for general sale but the same was being taken to the factory where the petitioner was a supervisor, for a tea party. Learned counsel further submitted that merely on the basis of the report of public analyst, it cannot be said that the petitioner had committed the alleged crime or that the milk was 'adulterated'. Lastly, on the strength of the aforesaid arguments and the facts and circumstances of the above case, learned counsel submitted that since the incident in the present case relates to the year 1996 and since the petitioner does not have any criminal antecedents, therefore, the present revision petition deserves acceptance. Learned counsel for the petitioner in the alternative submitted that the occurrence relates to the year 1996 and the petitioner has already served some part of the sentence awarded to him, therefore, the substantive sentence awarded to the petitioner may be reduced to the period already undergone by him. [2024:RJ-JD:13596] (3 of 4) [CRLR-981/2002] Reliance was placed on the judgment of the Hon'ble Supreme Court of India in the case of Puttaswamy v State of Karnataka: 2009 (1) WLC (SC) (Cri.) 623 and a judgment of the Coordinate Bench of this Court in the case of Kamla Prasad v. State of Rajasthan: 2014 CrilLJ 2582. Per contra, learned Public Prosecutor submitted that there is no illegality or infirmity in the impugned judgments whatsoever and therefore, the same do not call for any interference in exercise of revisional jurisdiction by this Court.",
